# Line-ending and diff handling.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# core.autocrlf is true on the machines this is developed on, so Git rewrites
# LF to CRLF on checkout for anything it considers text. It decides that by
# looking for a NUL byte in the first 8000 bytes: images and model weights have
# one almost immediately and are left alone, which is why 130-odd binaries have
# been safe here without this file.
#
# A PDF does not. Its header, metadata and cross-reference table are ASCII, and
# the first compressed stream can sit past the 8000-byte mark — so Git called
# docs/Seventh-AI-Vision-Features.pdf text and warned it would convert the 194
# LF bytes inside it on the next checkout. That is a corrupt PDF for anyone
# cloning fresh, and it would have been discovered by whoever opened the file
# rather than by anything that runs in CI.
#
# The rest are declared defensively. They are safe today only because their
# format happens to put a NUL early; saying so explicitly costs nothing and
# does not depend on that staying true.

# Documents
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.pptx  binary

# Images
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.bmp   binary
*.svgz  binary

# Fonts
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary

# Media
*.mp4   binary
*.webm  binary
*.mov   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary

# Models and archives
*.onnx  binary
*.pt    binary
*.pth   binary
*.engine binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.tar   binary
*.7z    binary

# Shell scripts must keep LF whatever the host does — a .sh with CRLF fails
# inside a Linux container with a "bad interpreter" error that names the wrong
# problem. Same for the entrypoints this repo ships to Docker.
*.sh    text eol=lf
docker/ssl-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
